# Rolls-Royce 20/25 coachbuilt saloon, 1929-1936.
#
# Rolls-Royce's "small" car and the best-selling Rolls of the inter-war years:
# 3,827 chassis built 1929-36, succeeding the 20 hp and replaced by the 25/30.
# 3,669 cc overhead-valve six, four-speed manual, servo-assisted four-wheel
# drum brakes, semi-elliptic springs. The body was coachbuilt to the owner's
# order; the modelled form is the closed saloon used by Park Ward, Hooper and
# Rippon, with an upright radiator, long bonnet, separate wings and running
# boards.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rolls-Royce_20/25
# https://www.supercars.net/blog/1929%E2%86%921936-rolls-royce-2025/
# https://www.christies.com/en/lot/lot-244308
#
# Dimensions: 4,570 mm long, 1,765 mm wide, about 1,800 mm high, wheelbase
# 3,270 mm (3,350 mm from 1930).
#
# Colours: most 20/25 coachwork was black; dark maroon and dark blue were common
# alternatives and cream or two-tone schemes were offered by the coachbuilders.
# The four modelled colours are the period's usual choices, not a factory list
# (there was no factory body colour list: the coachbuilder painted the body).
#
# Weight: 3,827 chassis over seven years make this the most numerous Rolls of the
# era but a very rare car against the total parc; set at 6 in total (black 2,
# maroon 2, blue 1, cream 1) so the Very-high class has a car without distorting
# ordinary traffic. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 100 km/h: a game-balance figure; the period claim was about 76 mph.
# Comfort 150/405: a long-wheelbase coachbuilt saloon with soft suspension and a
# quiet six; the line gives 405.
#
# Dates describe road presence: 1929 to the late 1940s, when pre-war cars were
# still in use.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/rolls-royce-20-25.blend in the blends repository.
# References: citycars/references/rolls-20-25/ (gitignored).
# Image credits: 1932 Rippon saloon and 1933 saloon photographs via Wikimedia
# Commons (see reference file names for the photographers and licences).

obj=citycar
name=RollsRoyce2025(Black)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=2
intro_year=1929
intro_month=1
retire_year=1948
retire_month=6
speed=100
class=4
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=150
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=405

